# Git & Versioning Standards

## Commit Messages

Use [Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/) format:

```
type(scope): short description

Optional longer body explaining WHY, not what.
```

Types: `feat`, `fix`, `docs`, `refactor`, `test`, `chore`, `build`, `ci`
Scopes: `agents`, `rag`, `api`, `frontend`, `config`, `docker` (optional, use when change is scoped)

```
feat(rag): add hybrid retrieval with BM25 reranking
fix(agents): handle missing chunks in tutor node fallback
docs: add ADR-0003 for ChromaDB collection strategy
refactor(api): extract pagination into shared dependency
```

## Commit Granularity

- One logical unit of work per commit (a feature, a fix, a refactor -- not a mix)
- If a feature touches backend + frontend, that's still one commit if they're coupled
- Never commit half-working code; each commit should leave the project in a runnable state

## Branching

- `master` is the main branch -- always deployable
- Solo mode (current default): direct commits to `master` are allowed for speed
- Team mode (when contributors join): use feature branches `feat/short-name` and `fix/short-name`, then merge via PR
- If a risky change is expected (schema migration, major refactor), prefer a feature branch even in solo mode

## Never Commit

- `.env` files or API keys (use `.env.example` for templates)
- Uploaded PDFs or user data (`uploads/`, `data/`)
- Large binary files
- `node_modules/`, `__pycache__/`, `.venv/`

## Versioning

Semantic versioning for releases: `vMAJOR.MINOR.PATCH`
- `v0.x.x` during initial development (pre-1.0)
- Bump MINOR for new features, PATCH for fixes
- Tag releases: `git tag v0.1.0`
- Each release gets a dated section in `CHANGELOG.md` moved from `[Unreleased]`
